CHUNK({"chunk":"K","module":"m4","topics":[{"id":"m4-eia-basics","title":"Environmental Impact Assessment: meaning and principles","short":"EIA basics","blurb":"What EIA is, why it matters, its six objectives and its six key principles.","source":"Lecture W4 (Environmental Impact Assessment), pages 1–2","tabs":[{"title":"What it is","html":"\n          <p class=\"lead\"><b>Environmental Impact Assessment (EIA)</b> is a process to evaluate the likely environmental consequences of a proposed project <b>before it begins</b>, to identify and mitigate adverse impacts, including <b>social, cultural and human health</b> factors.</p>\n          <table class=\"tbl\">\n            <thead><tr><th>Why it matters</th><th></th></tr></thead>\n            <tbody>\n              <tr><td><b>Environmental protection</b></td><td>Ecosystems, biodiversity, natural resources</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Public health</b></td><td>Impacts on human health and well-being</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Sustainable development</b></td><td>Balances economic growth with protection</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Informed decisions</b></td><td>Gives decision-makers the facts</td></tr>\n            </tbody>\n          </table>\n        "},{"title":"Objectives","html":"\n          <ol>\n            <li><b>Identify</b> potential impacts, positive and negative.</li>\n            <li><b>Predict</b> their magnitude and significance.</li>\n            <li><b>Evaluate alternatives</b> to the project.</li>\n            <li><b>Develop mitigation</b> to reduce or remove negative impacts.</li>\n            <li><b>Inform</b> decision-makers and the public.</li>\n            <li><b>Promote</b> environmentally sound, sustainable development.</li>\n          </ol>\n        "},{"title":"Principles","html":"\n          <table class=\"tbl\">\n            <tbody>\n              <tr><td><b>Participation</b></td><td>Involve stakeholders</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Transparency</b></td><td>Open, accessible information</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Accountability</b></td><td>Responsibility for outcomes</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Credibility</b></td><td>Reliable, objective assessment</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Sustainability</b></td><td>Long-term protection</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Integration</b></td><td>Environment built into every planning stage</td></tr>\n            </tbody>\n          </table>\n          <div class=\"mnemonic\">P-T-A-C-S-I: Participation, Transparency, Accountability, Credibility, Sustainability, Integration.</div>\n          <div class=\"note exam\"><p>For \"Explain EIA and its principles\": (1) the full definition, including that it happens before the project and covers social, cultural and health factors; (2) why it matters (protection, public health, sustainable development, informed decisions); (3) the six objectives; (4) the six principles, one line each. Name the eight process steps at the end if marks allow.</p></div>\n        "}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Define EIA.","back":"A process to evaluate a proposed project's environmental consequences <b>before it begins</b> and mitigate adverse impacts."},{"front":"Six principles of EIA?","back":"Participation, transparency, accountability, credibility, sustainability, integration."},{"front":"Name three objectives of EIA.","back":"Identify impacts, predict magnitude, evaluate alternatives (also mitigate, inform, promote sustainability)."}],"cardCount":4,"formulaCount":0},{"id":"m4-eia-process","title":"The EIA process","short":"EIA process","blurb":"The eight steps of EIA from screening to monitoring, with the purpose, process and outcome of each, and the mitigation hierarchy.","source":"Lecture W4 (Environmental Impact Assessment), pages 2–4","tabs":[{"title":"Eight steps","html":"\n          <div class=\"steps\">\n            <div><b>Screening</b>: does the project need a full EIA? Compare with mandatory lists (mining, highways, power plants); consider size, location (near sensitive areas), impacts. Outcome: required or not.</div>\n            <div><b>Scoping</b>: find the key issues; engage agencies, NGOs, public; set geographic, temporal and thematic boundaries; write the <b>Terms of Reference (ToR)</b>.</div>\n            <div><b>Impact assessment</b>: forecast environmental and socio-economic impacts; list them with magnitude, significance, duration.</div>\n            <div><b>Mitigation measures</b>: avoid, reduce or compensate.</div>\n            <div><b>Environmental Management Plan (EMP)</b>: how mitigation is carried out and monitored.</div>\n            <div><b>Public consultation</b>: hearings, community meetings, written feedback.</div>\n            <div><b>Review and decision</b>: regulators or expert committees judge the report.</div>\n            <div><b>Monitoring and evaluation</b>: track mitigation and its effect.</div>\n          </div>\n          <div class=\"mnemonic\">\"Some Scientists Insist Mitigation Ends Public Review Monitoring\": Screening, Scoping, Impact, Mitigation, EMP, Public, Review, Monitoring.</div>\n        "},{"title":"Impacts and mitigation","html":"\n          <p><b>Types of impact</b>: positive or negative, direct or indirect, short- or long-term, reversible or irreversible. <b>Tools</b>: checklists, matrices (<b>Leopold matrix</b>), modelling, GIS, risk assessment.</p>\n          <h4>Mitigation hierarchy</h4>\n          <ol>\n            <li><b>Avoid</b> (e.g. move the project).</li>\n            <li><b>Minimise</b> (e.g. control emissions).</li>\n            <li><b>Rehabilitate / restore</b> (e.g. replant vegetation).</li>\n            <li><b>Offset / compensate</b> (e.g. biodiversity offsets).</li>\n          </ol>\n          <p>Outcome: a mitigation plan built into project design.</p>\n        "},{"title":"EMP to monitoring","html":"\n          <table class=\"tbl\">\n            <tbody>\n              <tr><td><b>EMP</b></td><td>Components: monitoring plan, reporting, emergency response. Clear roles. Often a <b>legal condition of approval</b></td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Public consultation</b></td><td>Transparency; concerns addressed and documented</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Review</b></td><td>Criteria: scientific validity, adequacy of <b>baseline data</b>, appropriate mitigation. Outcome: approve, modify or reject</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Monitoring</b></td><td>Measurable indicators, regular reports, <b>adaptive management</b> (adjust mitigation from results)</td></tr>\n            </tbody>\n          </table>\n          <div class=\"note exam\"><p>For \"Describe the EIA process\": write the eight steps in order as headings. Under each give its purpose, how it is done and its outcome (e.g. screening ends in \"EIA required or not\", scoping ends in the ToR, review ends in approve, modify or reject). Add the four types of impact and the mitigation hierarchy with one example each. A flowchart of the eight boxes earns easy marks.</p></div>\n        "}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Eight steps of EIA?","back":"Screening, scoping, impact assessment, mitigation, EMP, public consultation, review and decision, monitoring."},{"front":"Purpose of screening?","back":"Decide whether a <b>full EIA is needed</b> and at what level."},{"front":"What does scoping produce?","back":"Key issues, study boundaries and the <b>Terms of Reference (ToR)</b>."}],"cardCount":6,"formulaCount":0},{"id":"m4-eia-methods","title":"EIA methodologies","short":"EIA methods","blurb":"Checklists, the Leopold matrix, network analysis and modelling, with their types, strengths and limits.","source":"Lecture W4 (Environmental Impact Assessment), pages 4–7","tabs":[{"title":"Checklists","html":"\n          <p class=\"lead\">A list of environmental parameters checked for whether, and how much, the project affects them.</p>\n          <table class=\"tbl\">\n            <tbody>\n              <tr><td><b>Simple descriptive</b></td><td>Lists factors (air, water, soil, noise); qualitative: YES/NO or LOW/MEDIUM/HIGH</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Questionnaire</b></td><td>Questions on actions: \"Will the project cause water pollution?\"</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Weighted</b></td><td>Numerical weights by importance; quantifies impact</td></tr>\n            </tbody>\n          </table>\n          <p><b>Pros</b>: easy, broad, little expertise. <b>Cons</b>: oversimplified, no interrelations, biased weights.</p>\n        "},{"title":"Matrix","html":"\n          <p class=\"lead\">A 2-D grid of project actions against environmental factors. The <b>Leopold matrix (1971)</b> is the most used.</p>\n          <ul>\n            <li><b>Rows</b>: environmental factors (air, water, flora, fauna).</li>\n            <li><b>Columns</b>: project actions (construction, drilling, transport).</li>\n            <li>Each cell: <b>magnitude</b> (1–10 or −10 to +10) and <b>importance</b> (1–10).</li>\n          </ul>\n          <p><b>Pros</b>: visual cause-effect, many impacts per activity. <b>Cons</b>: complex with many parameters, poor for cumulative or synergistic effects, semi-quantitative.</p>\n        "},{"title":"Network and models","html":"\n          <div class=\"vs\">\n            <div class=\"side\"><h4>Network analysis</h4><p>Maps the <b>cause-effect chain</b>: primary, secondary, tertiary impacts. Road → deforestation → erosion → river sedimentation → aquatic life hit. Good for <b>indirect and cumulative</b> impacts and can reveal links that checklists and matrices miss, so it suits scenario analysis. But it needs expertise and time, is not suitable for quick assessments, and can become highly complex and data-intensive.</p></div>\n            <div class=\"side\"><h4>Modelling</h4><p><b>Quantitative</b> computer simulations. Air models predict pollutant dispersion (Gaussian plume, <b>AERMOD</b>); water models simulate contamination of surface or groundwater (<b>QUAL2K, SWAT</b>); noise models estimate noise levels around the site; ecological models predict habitat loss and species distribution; <b>risk models</b> estimate the probability and consequences of environmental hazards. Good for \"what if\" scenario planning backed by scientific evidence; needs accurate baseline data and expertise, is sensitive to assumptions and input errors, and is costly and slow.</p></div>\n          </div>\n        "},{"title":"Comparison","html":"\n          <table class=\"tbl\">\n            <thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>Nature</th><th>Strength</th><th>Limit</th></tr></thead>\n            <tbody>\n              <tr><td>Checklist</td><td>Qualitative: says whether a factor is hit (YES/NO, LOW/HIGH)</td><td>Simple and gives broad coverage with little expertise</td><td>No cause-effect shown, because each factor is ticked on its own</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Matrix</td><td>Semi-quantitative: each cell scores magnitude and importance</td><td>Shows action-factor interactions with visual clarity</td><td>Cumbersome with many parameters and poor for cumulative effects</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Network</td><td>Qualitative/descriptive: a flowchart of linked impacts</td><td>Identifies indirect and cumulative impacts down the chain</td><td>Complex and needs expert knowledge and time</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Modelling</td><td>Quantitative: mathematical or computer simulation</td><td>Accurate predictions and \"what if\" scenario testing</td><td>Data-heavy and sensitive to assumptions and input errors</td></tr>\n            </tbody>\n          </table>\n          <div class=\"note exam\"><p>For \"Explain EIA methodologies\": name the four methods, then for each give the definition, how it works (checklist types; Leopold matrix rows, columns and cell scores; the road example for network; the five model types), its advantages and its limitations. Finish with this comparison table.</p></div>\n        "}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Four EIA methodologies?","back":"<b>Checklist, matrix, network analysis, modelling</b>."},{"front":"Most widely used matrix and its year?","back":"<b>Leopold matrix, 1971</b>."},{"front":"Leopold matrix rows and columns?","back":"Rows: <b>environmental factors</b>. Columns: <b>project actions</b>."}],"cardCount":6,"formulaCount":0},{"id":"m4-eia-sectors","title":"EIA in sectors, benefits and challenges","short":"EIA uses and limits","blurb":"How EIA works for infrastructure, energy, mining, agriculture, tourism and forestry, plus its benefits, challenges and future.","source":"Lecture W4 (Environmental Impact Assessment), pages 7–10","tabs":[{"title":"Sectors","html":"\n          <table class=\"tbl\">\n            <thead><tr><th>Sector</th><th>Key impacts</th><th>Mitigation examples</th></tr></thead>\n            <tbody>\n              <tr><td>Roads, railways</td><td>Air, water, noise, habitat fragmentation, displacement</td><td>Noise barriers, <b>wildlife crossings</b>, erosion control, resettlement</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Airports, ports</td><td>Coastal ecosystems, dredging, oil spills</td><td><b>Ballast water</b> plans, artificial reefs, alternative fuels</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Dams</td><td>Flow regimes, fish migration, flooding land</td><td><b>Environmental flows</b>, <b>fish ladders</b>, compensation</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Power plants</td><td>Air, water, GHGs, waste</td><td>Pollution control, <b>closed-loop cooling</b></td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Oil and gas</td><td>Land and marine ecosystems</td><td>Spill prevention and response</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Wind / solar</td><td>Birds and bats, noise, visuals / land and water</td><td>Siting, deterrents, water-efficient tech</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Mining</td><td>Open-pit: land, water, air; underground: groundwater, stability; mineral processing: water, air, waste</td><td>Erosion control, restoration, closure plans, prevent <b>acid mine drainage</b>; closed-loop water systems for processing plants</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Agriculture</td><td>Irrigation: <b>salinity</b>; livestock: GHGs; land conversion</td><td>Efficient irrigation, manure plans, anaerobic digesters</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Tourism</td><td>Resorts, ecotourism, infrastructure</td><td>Wastewater treatment, protect reefs, <b>limit visitors</b></td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Forestry</td><td>Logging, reforestation, conversion</td><td>Selective logging, <b>native species</b>, avoid monocultures and old-growth conversion</td></tr>\n            </tbody>\n          </table>\n        "},{"title":"Benefits and challenges","html":"\n          <div class=\"vs\">\n            <div class=\"side\"><h4>Benefits</h4><ul>\n              <li><b>Improved project design</b> and environmental performance, because problems are found while the plan can still change.</li>\n              <li><b>Reduced impacts and risks</b>, since mitigation is built into the design.</li>\n              <li><b>Stakeholder engagement</b> and public acceptance, because people are heard before approval.</li>\n              <li><b>Transparency and accountability</b>: the report and decision are open to scrutiny.</li>\n              <li><b>Sustainable development</b>: growth is balanced with protection.</li>\n              <li><b>Cost savings</b> from early identification of problems, which is cheaper than fixing damage later.</li>\n            </ul></div>\n            <div class=\"side\"><h4>Challenges</h4><ul>\n              <li><b>Lack of capacity</b>: many developing countries lack the technical and financial capacity for thorough EIAs.</li>\n              <li><b>Limited public participation</b>, so projects may not address community concerns.</li>\n              <li><b>Weak enforcement</b> of EIA rules, which undermines the whole process.</li>\n              <li><b>Cumulative impacts</b>: EIA looks at one project at a time and misses the combined effect of many projects in the same area.</li>\n            </ul></div>\n          </div>\n          <p><b>Future</b>: trends are more <b>Strategic Environmental Assessment (SEA)</b>, cumulative impact assessment and climate change considerations; innovation through GIS and remote sensing; and integration of EIA with other sustainability assessment tools.</p>\n          <div class=\"note exam\"><p>For \"Uses of EIA in different sectors\" or \"Benefits and challenges of EIA\": go sector by sector (infrastructure, energy, mining, agriculture, tourism, forestry), giving the key impacts and one or two mitigation measures for each. Then list the six benefits and the four challenges with their reason, and close with the future trends (SEA, GIS, integration).</p></div>\n        "}],"flashcards":[{"front":"EIA mitigation for dams?","back":"<b>Environmental flows, fish ladders</b>, compensation for displaced people."},{"front":"EIA concern for wind farms?","back":"<b>Birds and bats</b>, noise, visual aesthetics."},{"front":"Four challenges of EIA?","back":"Lack of capacity, limited participation, weak enforcement, ignored cumulative impacts."}],"cardCount":5,"formulaCount":0}],"figures":[]});
